(* Quest Self-Hosted Compiler: The SSA's Value Numbering Interface
 *
 * Performs global value numbering along the dominator tree (docs/ssa.md
 * section 4.1, following Briggs, Cooper, and Simpson) to eliminate redundant
 * computations. When a pure instruction or load is dominated by an equivalent
 * one (a block dominates another when every path from entry passes through
 * it), the later instruction is replaced by the earlier result. Equivalence
 * compares opcode, immediate, symbol, type, and operand value numbers (with
 * constants compared by value, and load addresses decomposed into base,
 * index, and constant offset).
 *
 * Non-invariant loads are deduplicated only while no store or writing call
 * intervenes, and an 8-byte store forwards its stored value to subsequent
 * loads of that address. Copies are replaced by their argument, and trivial
 * block parameters (ones every incoming jump binds to the same value or to
 * the parameter itself) are replaced by that value.
 *
 * Several kinds of instructions are deduplicated only within their own block:
 * comparisons, bitwise ands, multiplications, and pointer additions (which
 * arm64 instruction selection fuses into their users within a block);
 * constants, symbol addresses, and nulls (which otherwise stay live across
 * blocks and increase register spills); and 8-byte loads whose neighboring
 * word is also loaded in the same block (so arm64 can combine them into a
 * pair load). Finally, pure instructions, loads, and copies left with no uses
 * are removed. *)

interface ValueNumbers
import
    ssa = questlang/ssa/nodes : questlang/ssa/Nodes
export
    numberFunction(f: ssa.Function): Ok
    numberUnit(u: ssa.Unit): Ok
end
